      "status": "done",
      "result": {
        "summary": "Traced all six test_gate_timeout.py cases against gate.py's implementation: settings() correctly rejects non-int/bool/<=0 timeout_s and cleanup_timeout_s with fallback to defaults; run_bounded starts the child in its own session/process group and escalates SIGTERM->SIGKILL on timeout, killing the whole tree (matches test_timeout_kills_process_tree); run_gate retries exactly once, running cleanup_cmd only on a timed-out attempt and skipping it when unset (matches test_cleanup_command_runs_on_timeout and test_timeout_retries_once); the gates registry file is written on process start and unlinked in a finally block regardless of outcome, satisfying test_fast_gate_unaffected's 'registry entry removed' and 'no notify/cleanup' checks; running_gates() correctly filters by elapsed_s > threshold*timeout_s and tolerates malformed registry files. Diff stays within the declared scope (gate.py, pool.toml [gate] table, test file only) and isn't wired into the daemon/cli, which is consistent with the task's scope list. The packet's own gate metadata (gate_reds: 0, first_green_at set) confirms tests-green.sh passed on this diff, satisfying acceptance criterion 7, despite an unrelated evidence note about out-of-scope shared-state flakiness from an earlier round. No defects found against the stated acceptance criteria.",
